["\n\n", -0.0003234671021346003], ["\n", -10.382609367370605]]}, {"t": "```", "lp": -9.64449645834975e-05, "top": [["```", -9.64449645834975e-05], ["`", -10.249505996704102]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.17295120656490326, "top": [["\n", -0.17295120656490326], ["bash", -1.852461338043213]]}, {"t": "options", "lp": -0.0050633372738957405, "top": [["options", -0.0050633372738957405], ["mod", -6.492967128753662]]}, {"t": " xe", "lp": -0.03562881052494049, "top": [[" xe", -0.03562881052494049], [" i", -4.133920192718506]]}, {"t": " c", "lp": -0.0006326650036498904, "top": [[" c", -0.0006326650036498904], [" force", -9.253377914428711]]}, {"t": "cs", "lp": -0.00020667066564783454, "top": [["cs", -0.00020667066564783454], ["cm", -9.264042854309082]]}, {"t": "_mode", "lp": -0.0008280128240585327, "top": [["_mode", -0.0008280128240585327], ["=", -9.141536712646484]]}, {"t": "=", "lp": -0.029485883191227913, "top": [["=", -0.029485883191227913], ["=N", -3.9876463413238525]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc under the Xe Driver\n\nThe `ccs_mode` (Compute and Copy Engine selection) parameter controls how the driver distributes work across the **compute engines (CCS)** and **copy engines (CCS)** available on your GPU. On the Arc A770, you have multiple CCS instances that the driver can pin work onto.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**CCS Mode 0 (default/auto)**\n- The driver automatically selects which compute/copy engine to use\n- Most portable option, lets the kernel make scheduling decisions\n\n**CCS Mode 1**\n- Pins work to a **specific subset** of compute engines\n- Typically uses the **first half** of available CCS engines\n- Gives you more deterministic control over which engine handles your workload\n\n**CCS Mode 2**\n- Pins work to a **different subset** of compute engines\n- Typically uses the **second half** of available CCS engines\n- Useful for isolating workloads onto specific engine groups\n\n**CCS Mode 4**\n- This is where it gets specific to your use case\n\n## When to Use Mode 4\n\nMode 4 is typically used when you want to **pin to a specific compute engine** or gain the finest-grained control over engine assignment. Common scenarios:\n\n- **Debugging** engine-specific issues or reproducing bugs on a particular CCS\n- **Performance tuning** where you've identified that certain engines perform better or have less contention\n- **Multi-process isolation** where you want to guarantee separate workloads don't share engines\n\n## Important Caveat\n\nThe exact semantics of each mode can vary between driver versions, and the numbering scheme has shifted over time as the Xe driver and Intel Arc support have evolved. The `ccs_mode` parameter is passed via kernel module options like:\n\n```\noptions xe ccs_mode="}